{"name":"Catch the Wave","hex":"#b5dcd8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"catch the wave","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To mount one's surfboard at a distance from shore, turn it toward land, and control it for a successful, satisfying ride on inrushing water.","To derive benefit from a fortunate opportunity; to experience a sudden increase in energy or luck."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/catch_the_wave"}],"entries":[{"title":"Catch the Wave","blocks":[{"text":"Catch the Wave  is an annual professional wrestling tournament promoted by the Pro Wrestling Wave promotion. It has been held since 2009 and takes place between the end of April and the beginning of August. As up until March 2013 there were no singles championships in Pro Wrestling Wave, Catch the Wave was effectively the top singles achievement in the promotion. The tournament is contested in round-robin format with the winners of each block advancing to the semifinals. From 2010 to 2012, the tournament included a \"Loser Revival\" battle royal, where already eliminated wrestlers battle for the fourth and final spot in the semifinals of the tournament. This system allowed the tournament to be won twice by a wrestler, who was originally eliminated in the round-robin section. From 2011 onwards, the tournament has concluded with an award ceremony. The winner of the main tournament earns the title of \"Nami Onna\"  and ¥1,000,000. Since 2014, the tournament occasionally held a separate competition for rookie wrestlers, running concurrently with the main blocks."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catch_the_Wave","wikidata":"Q5051724"},{"title":"New Coke","blocks":[{"text":"New Coke was the informal name given to a reformulated version of the soft drink Coca-Cola, introduced by the Coca-Cola Company in the United States in April 1985. It was renamed Coke II in 1990 and was discontinued in July 2002. By 1985, Coca-Cola had been losing market share to diet soft drinks and non-cola beverages for several years. Blind taste tests indicated that consumers preferred the sweeter taste of rival Pepsi-Cola, leading the company to reformulate its product."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fa/New_Coke_can.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Coke","wikidata":"Q41084"},{"title":"Wave","blocks":[{"text":"In mathematics and physical science, a wave is a propagating dynamic disturbance of one or more quantities. Periodic waves oscillate repeatedly about an equilibrium (resting) value at some frequency. When the entire waveform moves in one direction, it is said to be a traveling wave; by contrast, a pair of identical superimposed periodic waves traveling in opposite directions makes a standing wave. In a standing wave, the amplitude of vibration has nulls at some positions where the wave amplitude appears smaller or even zero."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg/330px-2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wave","wikidata":"Q37172"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/%E5%B1%B1%E4%B8%8B%E3%82%8A%E3%81%AA2020.jpg/500px-%E5%B1%B1%E4%B8%8B%E3%82%8A%E3%81%AA2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/%E5%B1%B1%E4%B8%8B%E3%82%8A%E3%81%AA2020.jpg/500px-%E5%B1%B1%E4%B8%8B%E3%82%8A%E3%81%AA2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%E5%B1%B1%E4%B8%8B%E3%82%8A%E3%81%AA2020.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/SHIMMER_Volume_85_-_Yumi_Ohka_01.jpg/500px-SHIMMER_Volume_85_-_Yumi_Ohka_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/SHIMMER_Volume_85_-_Yumi_Ohka_01.jpg/500px-SHIMMER_Volume_85_-_Yumi_Ohka_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASHIMMER_Volume_85_-_Yumi_Ohka_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/05/Newcoke_maxheadroom.jpg/250px-Newcoke_maxheadroom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/05/Newcoke_maxheadroom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/05/Newcoke_maxheadroom.jpg/250px-Newcoke_maxheadroom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Max Headroom print ad from the \"Catch the wave\" campaign","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANewcoke_maxheadroom.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Santos_E_et_al_Neuroimage_2014_.gif/500px-Santos_E_et_al_Neuroimage_2014_.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Santos_E_et_al_Neuroimage_2014_.gif/500px-Santos_E_et_al_Neuroimage_2014_.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Example of biological waves expanding over the brain cortex, an example of spreading depolarizations[3]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASantos_E_et_al_Neuroimage_2014_.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Half-open_pipe_wave.gif/500px-Half-open_pipe_wave.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Half-open_pipe_wave.gif/500px-Half-open_pipe_wave.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sound pressure standing wave in a half-open pipe playing the 7th harmonic of the fundamental (n = 4)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHalf-open_pipe_wave.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Superpositionprinciple.gif/500px-Superpositionprinciple.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Superpositionprinciple.gif/500px-Superpositionprinciple.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Animation of two waves, the green wave moves to the right while blue wave moves to the left, the net red wave amplitude at each point is the sum of the amplitudes of the individual waves. Note that f(x, t) + g(x, t) = u(x, t). ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASuperpositionprinciple.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Amplitudemodulation.gif/500px-Amplitudemodulation.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Amplitudemodulation.gif/500px-Amplitudemodulation.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Amplitude modulation can be achieved through f(x,t) = 1.00×sin(2π/0.10×(x−1.00×t)) and g(x,t) = 1.00×sin(2π/0.11×(x−1.00×t)). Only the resultant is visible to improve clarity of waveform.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmplitudemodulation.gif"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c7/Wave_opposite-group-phase-velocity.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c7/Wave_opposite-group-phase-velocity.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"A wave with the group and phase velocitie